.TH BOOT.CFG 5
.SH NAME
boot.cfg \- the UEFI loader's configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \eSIEOS\eBOOT.CFG
(a USB key's EFI partition)
.br
.B \eEFI\eSIEOS\eBOOT.CFG
(a drive SIEOS was installed on)
.br
.B /boot/boot.cfg
(the copy the installer starts from)
.SH DESCRIPTION
SIEOS's UEFI loader (EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI) reads
.B BOOT.CFG
from its own partition before it loads the kernel (KERNEL.BIN) and the boot
modules.
It is made by
.B make usb
(tools/mkusb.py) and by the installer.
Lines are
.IB key = value ;
other lines (a
.B #
comment) are ignored.
At most 2047 bytes.
.TP
.BI module= FILE
a boot module, in the same directory; one line each, in order,
.B init.elf
first (then con, usb, vblk, fs).
At least one is needed.
.TP
.BI screen= W x H
the screen size to set if the firmware offers it (default 1920x1080); else
the mode it uses.
.TP
.BI root= GUID
SIEOS's partition, by its unique GUID, passed to the kernel as a boot
option: the disk server takes that partition on whichever drive it is.
.TP
.BI options= OPTIONS
more kernel options, for example
.B cpus=1
(start at most that many CPUs) or
.B pcores=4
(pretend CPUs 0..3 are P-cores, the others E-cores).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# SIEOS's UEFI loader (boot/uefi): the boot modules, init first; the screen size
module=init.elf
module=con.elf
module=usb.elf
module=vblk.elf
module=fs.elf
screen=1920x1080
root=3f6c2a1e-8d4b-4b2a-9c1e-2f5d7a8b9c0d
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boot (7),
.BR vblk (4),
.BR install (8)
